=== cost-aware factor / AP-model comparison foil (Detzel–Novy-Marx–Velikov JF 2023) ===
cite: DOI 10.1111/jofi.13225 — ignoring costs biases tests toward high-turnover factors/models
slow_factor  turnover≈0.506  gross_SR≈-0.119  net_SR@10bps≈-1.929
fast_factor  turnover≈3.197  gross_SR≈0.363  net_SR@10bps≈-11.368
ranking_by_gross_picks=fast  ranking_by_net_picks=slow
teach: cost-blind gross rankings bias toward high-turnover factors; net-of-cost rankings can reverse — Detzel spirit.
paper: after costs, FF5 (esp. cash-profitability variants) beat high-cost q/six-factor style comparisons.

=== Novy-Marx & Velikov RFS 2016 costs×turnover taxonomy (next to Detzel) ===
cite: NBER w20721 https://www.nber.org/papers/w20721 — A Taxonomy of Anomalies and Their Trading Costs
cite: RFS 2016 29(1) 104–147 DOI 10.1093/rfs/hhv063
spirit: gross zoo ≠ net zoo; low-turnover anomalies mostly survive net; high-turnover mostly die
NOT a homework alpha list — taxonomy of *costs×turnover*, not a shopping list
bucket | turnover | gross_SR | net_SR@10bps | net_survives?
low_turnover(<50%/mo spirit): turn≈0.506 gross_SR≈-0.119 net_SR≈-1.929 survives=False
high_turnover(>50%/mo spirit): turn≈3.197 gross_SR≈0.363 net_SR≈-11.368 survives=False
teach: most low-turnover strategies keep significant net spreads when cost-mitigated; few high-turnover do
name-drop FIM capacity: Frazzini–Israel–Moskowitz Trading Costs (SSRN 3229719) — capacity↔turnover spirit; NO capacity-as-alpha table here
REJECT: Procedia / FactFin Sharpe recipes; treating taxonomy rows as homework alphas

=== Harvey–Sancetta–Zhao NBER w34898 (2026): t≥3 + local FDR ===
cite: https://www.nber.org/papers/w34898 — What Threshold Should be Applied to Tests of Factor Models?
next to HLZ 2016 + Detzel costs: valid thresholds need dependence, null shape, selection bias
lower bound: researchers should employ a t-statistic cutoff of at least 3.0
local FDR: probability null is true GIVEN the realized test statistic — a p-value cannot supply that
  noise_t≈1.2: two-sided_p≈0.2301  localFDR_sketch≈0.957
  borderline_t≈2.0: two-sided_p≈0.0455  localFDR_sketch≈0.668
  HLZ_bar_t≈3.0: two-sided_p≈0.0027  localFDR_sketch≈0.091
  planted_t=4.2: two-sided_p≈0.0000  localFDR_sketch≈0.003
quiz_foil: p-value ≠ P(null|data); local FDR targets the latter
costs still matter for economic significance after statistical hurdles (Detzel bridge)

=== Admission gates pointer (arXiv:2610.07701) + Hou–Xue–Zhang 2020 RFS ===
gates host: backtest-traps — injected-truth; helpful only for weak train t≲2.5; use EXCESS not absolute
cite HXZ: https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhy131 — Replicating Anomalies (RFS 2020)
foil next to HLZ + Harvey–Sancetta–Zhao: most published anomalies FAIL under common replication
  65% of 452 anomalies fail |t|≥1.96 (NYSE breakpoints + value-weight); 82% fail multiple-testing |t|≥2.78
  even 'successful' replications often much smaller economically than original reports
toy_mixture fail_|t|<1.96≈87%  fail_|t|<2.78≈97%  (order-of-magnitude foil; paper=65%/82%)
teach: replication failure rate is a prior — new factors need HLZ/HSZ hurdles + Detzel costs, not a blog t-stat
